<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix: Proofs for Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Reverse invariance) ‣ 4.1 CBFs for flow-based generative sampling ‣ 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (Distribution shift for guided sampling) ‣ 4.1 CBFs for flow-based generative sampling ‣ 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a></h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Reverse invariance) ‣ 4.1 CBFs for flow-based generative sampling ‣ 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> (Reverse invariance of guided sampling process)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first establish initial feasibility at the onset of the sampling process at <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, an arbitrary sample drawn from the noise distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, the constricting barrier function is:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{h}(x(T),T)=h(x(T))+\epsilon(x(T),T)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{h}(x(T),T)=h(x(T))+\epsilon(x(T),T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By the condition <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(x(T),T)\geq-h(x(T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(x(T),T)\geq-h(x(T))</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows immediately that <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(x(T),T)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(x(T),T)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(T)\in\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(T)\in\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we consider the evolution of the state <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> under the controlled dynamics (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). The key observation is that in our setting, we have full knowledge of the noise process: at each instant <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the noise realization <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is observed <em id="A1.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em> the control <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,\xi,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,\xi,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is synthesized. Consequently, the result follows if we can
prove reverse invariance for each
realization of the noise path <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi(t)\}_{t\in[0,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi(t)\}_{t\in[0,T]}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the controlled dynamics reduce to the following
deterministic time-varying ordinary differential equation</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}(t)=\tilde{f}(x,t):=f_{\theta}(x,t)+u(x,\xi(t),t)+g(t)\xi(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}(t)=\tilde{f}(x,t):=f_{\theta}(x,t)+u(x,\xi(t),t)+g(t)\xi(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For each such realization, we verify the conditions of the generalization of Nagumo’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blanchini and Miani, 2008</a>, Section 4.2.2)</cite> for the time-varying set <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(t)=\{x\mid\tilde{h}(x,t)\geq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(t)=\{x\mid\tilde{h}(x,t)\geq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Existence and uniqueness of solutions to (<a href="#A1.E18" title="In A.1 Proof of Theorem (Reverse invariance of guided sampling process) ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs for Theorem and Theorem ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) for
each realization of the noise process <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>
hold by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Øksendal, 2003</a>, Theorem 5.2.1)</cite>
By condition (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In Theorem 4.1 (Reverse invariance) ‣ 4.1 CBFs for flow-based generative sampling ‣ 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), for any <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(x,t)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(x,t)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the control <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,\xi(t),t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,\xi(t),t)</annotation></semantics></math> ensures</p>
<table id="A1.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla\tilde{h}(x,t)\cdot\tilde{f}(x,t)+\frac{\partial\tilde{h}(x,t)}{\partial t}\leq\gamma(\tilde{h}(x,t))=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla\tilde{h}(x,t)\cdot\tilde{f}(x,t)+\frac{\partial\tilde{h}(x,t)}{\partial t}\leq\gamma(\tilde{h}(x,t))=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where the equality <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\tilde{h}(x,t))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\tilde{h}(x,t))=0</annotation></semantics></math> follows from <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> being a class-<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> function evaluated at zero. Since (<a href="#A1.E18" title="In A.1 Proof of Theorem (Reverse invariance of guided sampling process) ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs for Theorem and Theorem ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) is a deterministic ODE for each fixed noise path, the generalization of Nagumo’s theorem applies directly, yielding reverse invariance of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>: if <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(T)\in\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(T)\in\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)\in\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)\in\tilde{\mathcal{C}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since this argument holds for every noise realization <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi(t)\}_{t\in[0,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi(t)\}_{t\in[0,T]}</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(x(t),t)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(x(t),t)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>. At <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(x(0),0)=h(x(0))+\epsilon(x(T),0)=h(x(0))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(x(0),0)=h(x(0))+\epsilon(x(T),0)=h(x(0))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(0)\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(0)\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.  <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (Distribution shift for guided sampling) ‣ 4.1 CBFs for flow-based generative sampling ‣ 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> (Distributional shift wrt to guidance)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability measure associated with the unconstrained process governed by the drift <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> denote the measure associated with the controlled process governed by <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}+u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}+u</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the sampling process runs in reverse time from <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. To apply standard results, we reparametrize the process in forward time via <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=T-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=T-t</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> increases from <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> as sampling progresses. Under this substitution, the unconstrained and controlled SDEs become</p>
<table id="A1.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x=\bar{f}_{\theta}(x,\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau+\bar{g}(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}W_{\tau},\qquad\mathrm{d}x=[\bar{f}_{\theta}(x,\tau)+\bar{u}(x,\tau)]\,\mathrm{d}\tau+\bar{g}(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x=\bar{f}_{\theta}(x,\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau+\bar{g}(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}W_{\tau},\qquad\mathrm{d}x=[\bar{f}_{\theta}(x,\tau)+\bar{u}(x,\tau)]\,\mathrm{d}\tau+\bar{g}(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}W_{\tau},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}_{\theta}(x,\tau)=-f_{\theta}(x,T-\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}_{\theta}(x,\tau)=-f_{\theta}(x,T-\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{g}(\tau)=g(T-\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{g}(\tau)=g(T-\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{u}(x,\tau)=-u(x,T-\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{u}(x,\tau)=-u(x,T-\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard forward-time Wiener process. By Girsanov’s Theorem, the Radon-Nikodym derivative of the controlled measure <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the unconstrained measure <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A1.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}Q}{\mathrm{d}P}=\exp\left(\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}(x_{\tau},\tau)}{\bar{g}(\tau)}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}-\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}(x_{\tau},\tau)\|^{2}}{\bar{g}(\tau)^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}Q}{\mathrm{d}P}=\exp\left(\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}(x_{\tau},\tau)}{\bar{g}(\tau)}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}-\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}(x_{\tau},\tau)\|^{2}}{\bar{g}(\tau)^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The KL divergence is the expected log-likelihood ratio under <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q\,\|\,P)=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\ln\frac{\mathrm{d}Q}{\mathrm{d}P}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}-\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}\|^{2}}{\bar{g}^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>ln</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q\,\|\,P)=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\ln\frac{\mathrm{d}Q}{\mathrm{d}P}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}-\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}\|^{2}}{\bar{g}^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">By Girsanov’s Theorem, the process <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\tau}^{Q}=W_{\tau}-\int_{0}^{\tau}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\,\mathrm{d}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\tau}^{Q}=W_{\tau}-\int_{0}^{\tau}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\,\mathrm{d}s</annotation></semantics></math> is a Wiener process under <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}=\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}+\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}=\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}+\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#A1.E22" title="In A.2 Proof of Theorem (Distributional shift wrt to guidance) ‣ Appendix A Appendix: Proofs for Theorem and Theorem ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) yields</p>
<table id="A1.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q\,\|\,P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q\,\|\,P)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\left(\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}+\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right)-\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}\|^{2}}{\bar{g}^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\left(\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}+\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right)-\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}\|^{2}}{\bar{g}^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}\right]+\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}\|^{2}}{\bar{g}^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}\right]+\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{Q}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|\bar{u}\|^{2}}{\bar{g}^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\tau}^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\tau}^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Wiener process under <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, the stochastic integral <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\bar{u}}{\bar{g}}\cdot\mathrm{d}W_{\tau}^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a martingale under <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with zero expectation. Substituting back the original variables via <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bar{u}(x,\tau)\|^{2}=\|u(x,T-\tau)\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bar{u}(x,\tau)\|^{2}=\|u(x,T-\tau)\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{g}(\tau)^{2}=g(T-\tau)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{g}(\tau)^{2}=g(T-\tau)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and changing the integration variable back to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T-\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T-\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</p>
<table id="A1.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathrm{safe}}(0)\,\|\,p(0))=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathrm{safe}}(0)}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|u(x,t)\|^{2}}{g(t)^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}t\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>safe</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathrm{safe}}(0)\,\|\,p(0))=\frac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathrm{safe}}(0)}\left[\int_{0}^{T}\frac{\|u(x,t)\|^{2}}{g(t)^{2}}\,\mathrm{d}t\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">This concludes the proof.  <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Appendix: Additional experiments</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Spatial content constraints with alternate reference images</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ B.1 Spatial content constraints with alternate reference images ‣ Appendix B Appendix: Additional experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> demonstrates spatial and content-constrained image generation using red decorative pillows as the reference patch. Both constrained generations (b-c) preserve the pillows exactly at the specified location.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, the generated scenes exhibit predominantly light tones. This occurs because the reference patch (a) includes white pixels near its boundaries, which the diffusion model interprets as local context. The spatially-varying mask <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math> constrains boundary pixels to remain close to reference values, causing the model to generate ambient colors that transition naturally from the white boundary. This demonstrates that our CBF shield enforces constraints precisely as specified, including boundary characteristics. In practice, reference patches with neutral boundaries allow greater freedom in surrounding generation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.F6.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="162.88" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 485 162.88" width="485"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,162.88) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(262.78,0) translate(0,90.27)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -258.17 -66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:11.71em;--ltx-fo-height:9.54em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="132" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 132)" width="162"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.21429v1/figs/image_gen/reference_red_pillow.png" id="A2.F6.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:162/132;" width="162" height="132" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -186.01 -82.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.28em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="17.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">(a)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -68 -68)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.83em;--ltx-fo-height:9.83em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="136" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 136)" width="136"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.21429v1/figs/image_gen/pillow_outpaint_2.png" id="A2.F6.pic1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:136/136;" width="136" height="136" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -9.22 -82.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.33em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="18.45"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">(b)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 81.61 -68)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.83em;--ltx-fo-height:9.83em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="136" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 136)" width="136"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.21429v1/figs/image_gen/pillow_outpainting.png" id="A2.F6.pic1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:136/136;" width="136" height="136" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 141.15 -82.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.22em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="16.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">(c)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Spatial and content constrained generation with red pillow reference. (a) Reference patch (<math id="A2.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\times 50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\times 50</annotation></semantics></math> pixels) containing two red pillows with white background. (b) Generated image with 50 sampling steps. (c) Generated image with 200 sampling steps. Both preserve the pillows exactly while generating bedroom context. The white boundary pixels in the reference influence the surrounding color palette, resulting in light-toned ambient environments.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Constrained image generation with latent diffusion models</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate extending our CBF framework to latent diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, where the diffusion process operates in a compressed latent space rather than directly in pixel space. This extension is motivated by the scalability requirements of modern text-to-image models such as Stable Diffusion, which perform sampling in a low-dimensional latent space <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{4\times 64\times 64}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{4\times 64\times 64}</annotation></semantics></math> that is decoded to images in <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3\times 512\times 512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3\times 512\times 512}</annotation></semantics></math> via a variational autoencoder (VAE).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the VAE encoder and decoder, respectively, where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\ll n</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a constraint defined in image space via a barrier function <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <em id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent barrier function</em> as the composition</p>
<table id="A2.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}(z):=h(D(z))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}(z):=h(D(z)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which lifts the image-space constraint into the latent space via the decoder. The constricting barrier becomes <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(z,t)=\bar{h}(z)+\epsilon(z(T),t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(z,t)=\bar{h}(z)+\epsilon(z(T),t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the CBF condition for the latent dynamics <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}z=[f_{\theta}(z,t)+u]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}z=[f_{\theta}(z,t)+u]\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\mathrm{d}w</annotation></semantics></math> requires the gradient</p>
<table id="A2.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}\bar{h}(z)=\left(\frac{\partial D}{\partial z}\right)^{\top}\nabla_{x}h(x)\bigg|_{x=D(z)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}\bar{h}(z)=\left(\frac{\partial D}{\partial z}\right)^{\top}\nabla_{x}h(x)\bigg|_{x=D(z)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which is computed via a single backward pass through the decoder using automatic differentiation. The QP structure of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 4.2 Discrete-time implementation ‣ 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is preserved, with the state space replaced by <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply the spatial localization constraint from Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Constrained image generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.1 to Stable Diffusion v1.5 (<span id="A2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sd-legacy/stable-diffusion-v1-5</span>) from Hugging Face Diffusers. The prompt to the model is “high quality image of a rustic bedroom”. We constrain a rectangular region in the generated image to match a reference window patch, using the same per-pixel barrier function (<a href="#S5.E14" title="In 5.2.1 Location and content constraints ‣ 5.2 Constrained image generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) evaluated on the decoded image <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=D(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=D(z)</annotation></semantics></math>. The latent gradient (<a href="#A2.E26" title="In B.2 Constrained image generation with latent diffusion models ‣ Appendix B Appendix: Additional experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) is backpropagated through the VAE decoder at each sampling step.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ B.2 Constrained image generation with latent diffusion models ‣ Appendix B Appendix: Additional experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents the results. The CBF-guided latent diffusion model responds to the spatial constraint: a visually distinct region appears at the specified location, indicating that the latent-space control input successfully biases the sampling trajectory toward the constrained region. However, the reference window patch is not preserved with pixel-level fidelity. The constraint region appears blurred, color-shifted, and blended into the surrounding scene, in contrast to the exact preservation achieved in pixel-space experiments (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2.1 Location and content constraints ‣ 5.2 Constrained image generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ B.1 Spatial content constraints with alternate reference images ‣ Appendix B Appendix: Additional experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). This degradation stems from a fundamental limitation that the VAE decoder <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is not a diffeomorphism. The mapping from latent to image space is locally non-invertible and many-to-one, meaning that multiple latent codes can decode to similar but non-identical images. Consequently, enforcing <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}(z)=h(D(z))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}(z)=h(D(z))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> in latent space does not guarantee that the decoded image <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=D(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=D(z)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\hat{x})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\hat{x})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> with the same precision. The decoder introduces reconstruction artifacts that distort the constraint boundary, causing the gradient <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}\bar{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}\bar{h}</annotation></semantics></math> to point in directions that are approximately but not exactly aligned with the true image-space constraint.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.F7.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="231.75" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 440.44 231.75" width="440.44"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,231.75) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(220.22,0) translate(0,129.64)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -215.61 -97.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:14.09em;--ltx-fo-height:14.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="195" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 195)" width="195"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.21429v1/figs/image_gen/latent_cbf_result.png" id="A2.F7.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:195/195;" width="195" height="195" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.61 -97.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:14.09em;--ltx-fo-height:14.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="195" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 195)" width="195"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.21429v1/figs/image_gen/latent_cbf2.png" id="A2.F7.pic1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:195/195;" width="195" height="195" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -126.95 -121.57)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.28em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="17.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A2.F7.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">(a)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 108.89 -121.57)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.33em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="18.45"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A2.F7.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">(b)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Constrained image generation with latent diffusion models with prompt “high-quality image of a rustic bedroom”. The constraint is to produce the reference window in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2.1 Location and content constraints ‣ 5.2 Constrained image generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a) in the top right corner, same as in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Constrained image generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.1. We see that the model responds to the constraint by producing a visually distinct region at the target location, but the reference patch is not preserved with pixel-level fidelity due to the VAE decoder’s non-invertibility. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, the safety guarantee of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Reverse invariance) ‣ 4.1 CBFs for flow-based generative sampling ‣ 4 Guidance of flow-based models via constricting CBFs ‣ Provably Safe Generative Sampling with Constricting Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> holds with respect to the composed barrier <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}(z)=h(D(z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}(z)=h(D(z))</annotation></semantics></math>: if <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(z(0),0)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(z(0),0)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(D(z(0)))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(D(z(0)))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The issue is that the safe set <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{C}}=\{z\mid h(D(z))\geq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{C}}=\{z\mid h(D(z))\geq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math> in latent space does not correspond exactly to the intended safe set in image space when the decoder has non-trivial reconstruction error. This is not a failure of the CBF framework itself, but rather a limitation of the representation space in which the diffusion process operates.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
